/**

@page page_machines_ncc NCC

We recommend using `gpu10`, `gpu11` or `gpu12`, i.e. the A100 machines.
These cards have compute capability 8.0, so the GPU architecture is
`sm_80` throughout. Peano builds with CMake; its toolboxes and the ExaHyPE 2
extension are always built, so you only toggle the `PEANO_WITH_*` feature options.
NVC++ and the oneAPI software stack both work on this cluster.

# OpenMP offloading with Clang

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load nsight-systems cuda llvm
module load mvapich2/2.3.5-2

mkdir build
cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_80
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The `ompt` backend drives OpenMP target offloading. CMake adds the
matching `-fopenmp-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda --offload-arch=sm_80`
flags for you.

# OpenMP offloading with NVC++

On the A100 nodes you can also use the NVIDIA HPC compiler:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build
cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=nvc++ \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=nvc \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_80
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

CMake translates `sm_80` into the NVHPC `-mp=gpu -gpu=cc80` flags
automatically.

Some nodes (e.g. gpu3) have Titan XP which doesn't support OpenMP
offloading; only GPUs with compute capability >=70 can offload with
OpenMP. You can check the GPU model with `nvidia-smi -L`.

If runs crash with a complaint about a lack of visible devices, set the
following environment before you start your experiments:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# SYCL offloading with oneAPI

For Intel GPUs the oneAPI/`icpx` stack maps onto the `native` backend
with the `spir64` architecture:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh

mkdir build
cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=native \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=spir64
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

CMake emits the required `-fsycl -fsycl-targets=spir64` flags for you.
`icpx` uses a lower floating-point precision by default in
favour of performance; pass `-DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS=-fp-model=precise`
if you need stricter semantics.

To target the A100 cards through SYCL instead, use the AdaptiveCpp
backend, which compiles SYCL down to CUDA:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load cuda

mkdir build
cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=acpp \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_80
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

CMake sets the AdaptiveCpp target `cuda:sm_80` from `PEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH`.

When running the executable on the GPU, include `SYCL_DEVICE_FILTER=gpu`
before the terminal command to explicitly tell SYCL to run the code on
the GPU. In the case of a runtime error, it is also useful to add
`SYCL_PI_TRACE=2` to check where the error was raised.

*/
